// Monetary base (M0, FRED BOGMBASE), M2 money stock (FRED M2SL) and Fed balance sheet
// total assets (FRED WALCL) — US only for now (1965-11 to 2026-06, monthly, YoY %). M0 and
// M2 YoY % computed here directly from the raw monthly levels (t vs t-12); WALCL YoY % was
// already FRED-published as WALCL_PC1 (weekly), downsampled to monthly by taking each
// month's last weekly observation. Start date (Nov 1965) is set by M2SL's own earliest
// available level (Nov 1964) plus the 12-month lag needed to compute a YoY %; M0 itself goes
// back to 1959, so it's the M2 series that binds the start. WALCL only exists on FRED from
// Dec 2003 (the Fed didn't publish a weekly balance-sheet series before then), so the
// "cb" field is simply absent before 2003-12 — the balance-sheet line starts partway through
// the chart by data availability, not by choice. M1 deliberately excluded: the Fed's
// May-2020 redefinition (folding savings deposits in) created a ~3.4x one-month "jump" that
// is definitional, not real money creation, and would be misleading on a YoY-%% axis. Framed
// as growth rates (not indexed levels) so the three series — very different in scale — are
// directly comparable, and so the crisis interventions show up as exactly what they are: a
// spike in the pace of money/balance-sheet growth, not a change of units.
const monetaryCrisisPeriods = [
  { x1: "2008-01", x2: "2009-12", label: "Παγκόσμια χρηματοπιστωτική κρίση", color: "#4a1f24" },
  { x1: "2020-01", x2: "2021-12", label: "COVID-19", color: "#1f4a45" },
];
// m0idx/m2idx (in monetaryUS above): cumulative growth index, Νοέμβριος 1965 = 100, computed
// directly from the raw monthly levels (BOGMBASE, M2SL) — level(t)/level(base)*100. Plotted on
// a log scale so six decades of compounding (M0 ×92, M2 ×51 by mid-2026) stay readable on one
// axis: equal vertical distance = equal %% change, so the 2008/2020 interventions show up as
// a visible kink in slope, not just a bigger number. The Fed balance sheet (WALCL) is NOT
// included here — FRED only publishes WALCL_PC1 (YoY %% change), not the raw weekly level, and
// a cumulative index can't be reconstructed from a YoY series alone without assuming a shape
// for the missing week-to-week path, which would make it an estimate, not real data. Add it
// once the WALCL level series (not just its %% change) is available.
// Real Effective Exchange Rate (REER), CPI-based, OECD Main Economic Indicators via FRED.

